     1	# neurojit
     2	
     3	`neurojit` is a Python package designed to enhance Just-In-Time (JIT) defect prediction by leveraging insights from both neurophysiological and empirical developer reactions to code. Modern developers make changes based on their understanding of the existing code context, and the difficulty in comprehending these changes can increase the likelihood of human error, potentially introducing defects. NeuroJIT captures the understandability of each commit through features that correlate with developers’ cognitive and empirical responses to different code segments. These features have been shown to improve JIT defect prediction models by identifying defect-inducing commits more effectively. For more information, see our research paper *"NeuroJIT: Improving Just-In-Time Defect Prediction Using Neurophysiological and Empirical Perceptions of Modern Developers."*
     4	
     5	## Installation
     6	
     7	You can install the NeuroJIT package from PyPI using pip:
     8	
     9	```Shell
    10	$ pip install neurojit
    11	```
    12	To install with additional dependencies for replicating the research results:
    13	
    14	```Shell
    15	$ pip install neurojit[replication]
    16	```
    17	
    18	For more information about the replication, see our [NeuroJIT replication package](https://github.com/Verssae/NeuroJIT)
    19	
    20	### Example Usage
    21	1. Filtering Commits and Saving Method Changes
    22	
    23	    ```python
    24	    from neurojit.commit import MethodChangesCommit, Mining, Method
    25	
    26	    mining = Mining()
    27	    target_commit = mining.only_method_changes(repo="activemq", commit_hash="8f40a7")
    28	    if target_commit is not None:
    29	        mining.save(target_commit)
    30	    ```
    31	2. Calculating Commit Understandability Features
    32	
    33	    Compute commit understandability features from the saved `MethodChangesCommit` instances.
    34	
    35	    ```python
    36	    from neurojit.cuf.metrics import CommitUnderstandabilityFeatures
    37	
    38	    cuf_calculator = CommitUnderstandabilityFeatures(target_commit)
    39	    features = ["HV","DD", "MDNL", "NB", "EC", "NOP", "NOGV", "NOMT", "II", "TE", "DD_HV"]
    40	    for feature in features:
    41	        value = getattr(cuf_calculator, feature)
    42	        print(f"{feature}: {value}")
    43	    ```
    44	
    45	3. Splitting the Dataset Chronologically with KFoldDateSplit
    46	
    47	    Use `KFoldDateSplit` to split the dataset into training and testing sets, considering chronological order, verification latency, and concept drifts.
    48	
    49	    ```python
    50	    from neurojit.tools.data_utils import KFoldDateSplit
    51	
    52	    data = pd.read_csv("...your jit-sdp dataset...")
    53	    data["date"] = pd.to_datetime(data["date"])
    54	    data = data.set_index(["date"])
    55	
    56	    splitter = KFoldDateSplit(
    57	        data, k=20, start_gap=3, end_gap=3, is_mid_gap=True, sliding_months=1
    58	    )
    59	
    60	    for i, (train, test) in enumerate(splitter.split()):
    61	        X_train, y_train = train[features], train["buggy"]
    62	        X_test, y_test = test[features], test["buggy"]
    63	    ```
